(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["embeddings",{"c:text-embeddings-and-semantic-similarity":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An \u003cb>embedding\u003c/b> is a list of numbers (a vector) that a model produces for a piece of text, trained so that texts with similar meaning point in similar directions. You compare two embeddings with \u003cb>cosine similarity\u003c/b>: close to 1 means \"about the same thing\", close to 0 means unrelated. Search by meaning is then simple: embed the query and find the stored vectors closest to it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>From text to a vector\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An \u003cb>embedding model\u003c/b> is a neural network, usually a transformer encoder and increasingly a small LLM, that reads a text (up to its maximum input, typically 512 to 32,000 tokens), combines the per-token states into one vector (\u003cb>pooling\u003c/b>), and returns a fixed number of floating-point values: 384, 768, 1,024 or 3,072 are common. No single number means anything readable. Meaning lives in the geometry: which vectors are near which.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The models are trained \u003cb>contrastively\u003c/b>. They see pairs that should match (a question and the passage that answers it, a title and its article, two paraphrases) next to many pairs that should not, and are pushed to score the matching pair higher. That is why embeddings capture \u003ci>topical relatedness\u003c/i>. They do not check truth, and they do not reliably know whether a passage \u003ci>answers\u003c/i> a question.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Calling one is a single request; the same OpenAI-compatible shape works for most providers and for local servers such as Ollama:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # or OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")\nresp = client.embeddings.create(\n    model=\"&lt;embedding-model&gt;\",\n    input=[\"How do I get a refund?\", \"Our returns policy\", \"Delivery times\"],\n)\nvectors = [d.embedding for d in resp.data]\nprint(len(vectors), len(vectors[0]))  # 3 vectors, e.g. 1536 numbers each\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three ways to compare two vectors\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Measure\u003c/th>\u003cth>Formula\u003c/th>\u003cth>Range\u003c/th>\u003cth>Says\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Dot product\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a·b = Σ aᵢ × bᵢ\u003c/td>\u003ctd>any number\u003c/td>\u003ctd>direction and length together\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cosine similarity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>a·b / (‖a‖ × ‖b‖)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>-1 to 1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>direction only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Euclidean (L2) distance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>√Σ (aᵢ - bᵢ)²\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0 upwards\u003c/td>\u003ctd>smaller means closer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>‖a‖ is the \u003cb>length\u003c/b> (L2 norm) of a: the square root of the sum of its squared values. A worked example with three dimensions (real vectors have hundreds, but the arithmetic is identical):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>query q = [0.9, 0.1, 0.3]   \"refund policy\"\ndoc A   = [0.8, 0.2, 0.4]   \"how to get your money back\"\ndoc B   = [0.1, 0.9, 0.2]   \"delivery times\"\n\nq·A = 0.9×0.8 + 0.1×0.2 + 0.3×0.4 = 0.72 + 0.02 + 0.12 = 0.86\n‖q‖ = √(0.81 + 0.01 + 0.09) = √0.91 = 0.9539\n‖A‖ = √(0.64 + 0.04 + 0.16) = √0.84 = 0.9165\ncos(q, A) = 0.86 / (0.9539 × 0.9165) = 0.86 / 0.8743 = 0.984\n\nq·B = 0.09 + 0.09 + 0.06 = 0.24\n‖B‖ = √(0.01 + 0.81 + 0.04) = √0.86 = 0.9274\ncos(q, B) = 0.24 / (0.9539 × 0.9274) = 0.24 / 0.8847 = 0.271\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Document A ranks far above B, as you would hope, even though \"refund\" and \"money back\" share no words.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Normalisation: why the three measures can agree\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>L2 normalisation\u003c/b> divides a vector by its length so that its length becomes 1. For unit-length vectors, cosine similarity equals the dot product, and squared Euclidean distance equals 2 - 2 × cosine. All three measures then produce the same ranking. In the example, q and A normalised are [0.9435, 0.1048, 0.3145] and [0.8729, 0.2182, 0.4364]; their dot product is 0.984, the same as the cosine.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many providers return unit-length vectors already: the OpenAI guide says its embeddings are normalised to length 1, and Ollama's \u003ccode>/api/embed\u003c/code> endpoint returns L2-normalised vectors. Then use the dot product, which is the cheapest. If a model does not normalise, do it yourself once at write time and once at query time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When vectors are \u003ci>not\u003c/i> normalised, the measures disagree. Take C = 2 × A = [1.6, 0.4, 0.8]. Its cosine with q is still 0.984, but the dot product doubles to 1.72 and the Euclidean distance grows from 0.17 to 0.91. Some models are trained for dot product and use length to encode something extra. Use the metric on the model card, and configure the same metric in your index.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What a similarity score does not tell you\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Similar is not relevant.\u003c/b> \"How do I cancel my subscription?\" and \"How do I start my subscription?\" can score very close. So can a sentence and its negation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scores are model-specific.\u003c/b> A 0.8 from one model may be a 0.5 from another. Never mix vectors from different models, or even different versions of one model, in one index. Switching models means re-embedding everything.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fixed thresholds are fragile.\u003c/b> Prefer ranking (take the top k). If you need a cut-off, for example \"no good match, so say I don't know\", calibrate it on labelled examples.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Some models are asymmetric.\u003c/b> They expect different inputs for queries and documents: E5 models want \u003ccode>query: \u003c/code> and \u003ccode>passage: \u003c/code> prefixes, Cohere takes \u003ccode>input_type=\"search_query\"\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>\"search_document\"\u003c/code>, Qwen3 embedding models take an instruction on the query side. Skipping these silently costs quality.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Embeddings power more than RAG: near-duplicate detection (pairs above roughly 0.95 cosine, tuned on your data), clustering support tickets by topic, nearest-centroid classification with a handful of labels, recommendations, and semantic caching (the Reliability module). They are cheap: the OpenAI guide's pricing table implies about $0.02 per million tokens for its small model, as of late 2026. Record the model name, version and dimensions next to every stored vector.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you embed 20 sentences and show a similarity heatmap, and what pattern should you expect?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Write 20 sentences in four topics of five (say refunds, delivery, passwords, pricing), embed them in one batch call, L2-normalise the vectors, compute the 20 × 20 matrix S = E × Eᵀ (each cell is a cosine similarity) and plot it with matplotlib's \u003ccode>imshow\u003c/code>. Expect a diagonal of 1.0, four bright 5 × 5 blocks along the diagonal where same-topic pairs score higher than cross-topic pairs, the highest off-diagonal values for paraphrases, and some surprises such as a sentence and its negation scoring high, because embeddings measure topic rather than truth.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Compute the cosine similarity of [1, 0, 1] and [1, 1, 0].\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The dot product is 1×1 + 0×1 + 1×0 = 1. Both lengths are √2 ≈ 1.414, so the cosine is 1 / (1.414 × 1.414) = 1 / 2 = 0.5.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your vectors are unit length. Why can you use the dot product instead of cosine?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Cosine is the dot product divided by the product of the lengths; when both lengths are 1 the division changes nothing, so the dot product gives identical scores and rankings and is cheaper to compute.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must you re-embed the whole corpus when you change embedding model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each model has its own vector space, so a query vector from the new model cannot be meaningfully compared with document vectors from the old one; even the dimensions may differ. Mixed indexes silently return poor results.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Try it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build the heatmap from the first question. With Ollama you can run it locally with an embedding model such as \u003ccode>embeddinggemma\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nsentences = [...]  # your 20 sentences, grouped by topic\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\")\nE = np.array([d.embedding for d in client.embeddings.create(model=\"embeddinggemma\", input=sentences).data])\nE = E / np.linalg.norm(E, axis=1, keepdims=True)\nplt.imshow(E @ E.T, cmap=\"viridis\"); plt.colorbar()\nplt.yticks(range(len(sentences)), [s[:35] for s in sentences], fontsize=6)\nplt.savefig(\"heatmap.png\", dpi=150, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>","c:choosing-an-embedding-model-with-mteb":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the \u003cb>MTEB\u003c/b> leaderboard to shortlist three or four embedding models that suit your language, domain, size and licence, then \u003cb>decide with a retrieval eval on your own documents and queries\u003c/b>. Dimensions, maximum input length, price and hosting matter as much as a one-point difference in benchmark score.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What MTEB measures\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The \u003cb>Massive Text Embedding Benchmark\u003c/b> (Muennighoff et al., 2022) tests embedding models across 8 task types (retrieval, reranking, classification, clustering, pair classification, semantic textual similarity, summarisation and bitext mining), originally 58 datasets in 112 languages. Its headline finding still holds: no single model is best at every task. In 2025 the community extension \u003cb>MMTEB\u003c/b> grew it to more than 500 tasks in over 250 languages, and found that the best publicly available model on its multilingual benchmark at the time was multilingual-e5-large-instruct with only 560 million parameters, ahead of LLM-sized models with billions. Bigger is not automatically better.\u003c/p>\u003cp>How to read the leaderboard:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Look at the \u003cb>retrieval\u003c/b> score for search and RAG, not the overall average. A model that excels at classification may be mediocre at retrieval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Choose the benchmark for \u003cb>your language\u003c/b>. English-only scores say little about German or Hindi.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat differences of a point or two as noise for your data. Some models were trained on data close to the benchmark's, which inflates their scores (the same contamination problem as LLM benchmarks).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The criteria that matter\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Criterion\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why it matters\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example check\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval quality on your data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The only score that predicts your results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>recall@10 on 50+ of your queries\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Languages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multilingual models handle mixed corpora and cross-lingual queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Test queries in each language you serve\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Domain\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Code, legal and medical text use vocabulary general models handle poorly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Include domain jargon in the eval\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Maximum input tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Longer chunks are silently truncated\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare your longest chunk with the limit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Dimensions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Storage and search cost grow linearly with them\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1M vectors × 3,072 floats × 4 bytes = 12.3 GB\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Matryoshka support\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lets you shorten vectors with little loss\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Evaluate at full and reduced dimensions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hosting and licence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>API or open weights; data rules; commercial use\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Licence on the exact model card\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost and latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ingestion is a one-off bulk job; queries are per request\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Price per million tokens; p95 query latency\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Matryoshka embeddings: shorter vectors on demand\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Matryoshka Representation Learning\u003c/b> (Kusupati et al., 2022) trains a model so that the \u003ci>first\u003c/i> n dimensions of its vector are themselves a good embedding, like nested dolls. You can keep the first 256 of 1,024 dimensions, re-normalise, and lose only a little quality. The paper reports up to 14 times smaller embeddings at the same accuracy on its image benchmarks.\u003c/p>\u003cp>OpenAI's text-embedding-3 models support this through a \u003ccode>dimensions\u003c/code> request parameter, and its guide notes that text-embedding-3-large shortened to 256 dimensions still outperformed the older ada-002 model at its full 1,536 dimensions on MTEB. Many open models (for example the Qwen3 embedding series, which accepts output sizes from 32 to 1,024) support it too. If you truncate vectors yourself, normalise them again afterwards.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Storage arithmetic for 1 million vectors stored as 4-byte floats:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>3,072 dims: 1,000,000 × 3,072 × 4 bytes = 12.3 GB\n1,024 dims: 1,000,000 × 1,024 × 4 bytes =  4.1 GB\n  256 dims: 1,000,000 ×   256 × 4 bytes =  1.0 GB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The landscape as of late 2026\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Model versions change every few months, so treat names as examples. \u003cb>API models\u003c/b> come from OpenAI (text-embedding-3 small and large), Cohere (Embed), Voyage, Google (Gemini embeddings) and Jina. \u003cb>Open-weight models\u003c/b> include the Qwen3 embedding series (0.6B, 4B and 8B parameters, Apache 2.0, 32k-token input), Google's EmbeddingGemma (about 300M parameters, aimed at on-device use), BGE-M3 (dense, sparse and multi-vector output, 100+ languages, inputs up to 8,192 tokens), the multilingual E5 family, nomic-embed, Snowflake Arctic Embed and mixedbread models. Small open models run on a CPU; the larger ones want a GPU (see the self-hosting lesson).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Deciding with your own eval\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Build 50 to 200 real queries, each labelled with the documents or chunks that answer it (the measuring lesson shows how).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Embed your corpus with each shortlisted model, using its recommended query and document prefixes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare recall@10 and nDCG@10, plus cost per million tokens and query latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pick the cheapest model within noise of the best, and record why.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>When a general model plateaus on specialised text, fine-tuning an open embedding model on a few thousand (query, relevant passage) pairs from your domain is a well-trodden path with sentence-transformers. It is worth it only after hybrid search and reranking.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-warn\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Watch out\u003c/p>\u003cp>Changing embedding model means re-embedding and re-indexing everything. Keep the model and version as part of the index name (for example \u003ccode>docs_qwen3-0.6b_v1\u003c/code>) so you can build the new index next to the old one, compare, and switch over without downtime.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you shortlist three embedding models for your language and domain?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On the MTEB leaderboard, select the benchmark for your language (or the multilingual one), sort by retrieval score, and filter out models whose licence, hosting, maximum input length or size do not fit (for example open weights only, or under 1B parameters for CPU serving). Pick three that differ meaningfully, such as one API model, one small open model and one larger open model, and include any domain-specific model if one exists. Then run them all on 50 or more of your own labelled queries and compare recall@10, cost and latency before deciding.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a model's average MTEB score a poor guide for a RAG system?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The average mixes tasks such as classification and clustering that do not matter for search; the retrieval score on your language and, above all, results on your own documents and queries are what predict RAG quality.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does Matryoshka training let you do, and what must you do after truncating?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It lets you keep only the first n dimensions of a vector and still get a good embedding, trading a little quality for much less storage and faster search. After truncating you must L2-normalise the shortened vector again.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:approximate-nearest-neighbour-search-and-hnsw":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Exact search compares the query with every stored vector, which is too slow for millions of vectors. \u003cb>Approximate nearest neighbour (ANN)\u003c/b> indexes trade a little accuracy for a large speed-up. \u003cb>HNSW\u003c/b>, the default in most vector databases, is a layered graph you walk greedily from coarse to fine; its parameters \u003ccode>m\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ef_construction\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> set the balance between recall, speed and memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why exact search does not scale\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Exact (brute-force) k-nearest-neighbour search computes the similarity between the query and all N stored vectors, then keeps the top k. Each comparison costs d multiply-adds, where d is the dimension:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cost per query = N × d multiply-adds\n100,000 × 384      = 38 million  (about 10 ms on one CPU core)\n10,000,000 × 1,024 = 10 billion  (seconds on one core: 41 GB of vectors to read)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In this module's lab, exact search over 100,000 vectors of 384 dimensions took around 10 ms per query on one core. That is fine for small collections, or for a filtered subset of a few thousand rows. At tens of millions of vectors, or hundreds of queries per second, you need an index.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>ANN\u003c/b> indexes return \u003ci>most\u003c/i> of the true nearest neighbours, much faster. You measure the trade-off as \u003cb>ANN recall@k\u003c/b>: the share of the exact top k that the index returns. A recall of 0.95 at k = 10 means that on average 9.5 of the true 10 neighbours come back.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The main index families\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Family\u003c/th>\u003cth>Idea\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\u003cth>Trade-off\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Graph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Link each vector to its neighbours; walk the graph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>HNSW, DiskANN (Vamana)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Best speed and recall; memory-hungry; slow to build\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inverted file (IVF)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cluster vectors with k-means; search only the nearest clusters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Faiss IVF, pgvector ivfflat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast to build, less memory; needs training; lower recall at the same speed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Quantisation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compress vectors, compare compressed codes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Product quantisation, binary\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Huge memory savings; usually combined with the others\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trees and hashing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Partition space or hash similar vectors together\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Annoy, LSH\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Older; weaker in high dimensions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How HNSW works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>HNSW stands for \u003cb>Hierarchical Navigable Small World\u003c/b>. It combines two ideas.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>A navigable small-world graph.\u003c/b> Every vector is a node linked to a handful of its near neighbours. To search, start at an entry node, look at its neighbours, move to whichever is closest to the query, and repeat until no neighbour is closer. Because the graph also contains some longer links, a few hops cross large distances, like the \"six degrees of separation\" in social networks.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>A hierarchy, like a skip list.\u003c/b> Each node is assigned a top layer at random, with an exponentially decaying chance of reaching higher layers. The bottom layer holds every vector; each layer above holds a thinning sample, so its links span long distances.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Layer 2:  A ---------------------------- B             few nodes, long links\nLayer 1:  A ------- C --------- D ------ B             more nodes\nLayer 0:  A - E - C - F - G - D - H - I - B - J ...    every vector, short links\n\nsearch: enter at the top, greedily move towards the query,\ndrop a layer, repeat, then search layer 0 with a candidate list of size ef_search\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A search starts in the top layer, hops greedily towards the query, then drops down a layer and continues from where it arrived. On layer 0 it keeps a list of the \u003cb>ef_search\u003c/b> best candidates found so far and keeps exploring their neighbours until the list stops improving, then returns the best k. Insertion runs the same search with a list of size \u003cb>ef_construction\u003c/b> and links the new node to up to \u003cb>m\u003c/b> good, diverse neighbours on each of its layers. The paper shows the search cost grows roughly logarithmically with N.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The parameters\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Parameter\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it controls\u003c/th>\u003cth>Raise it for\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost\u003c/th>\u003cth>pgvector default\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>m\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Links per node per layer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Higher recall, especially at high dimension\u003c/td>\u003ctd>More memory, slower build\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ef_construction\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Candidate list size while building\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A better graph, higher recall\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slower build and inserts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Candidate list size while searching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Higher recall\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slower queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> is the one you tune at query time, often per request. It must be at least k. In the lab for this module, on 100,000 vectors, raising \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> from 10 to 32 lifted recall@10 from about 0.83 to 0.99 while queries stayed under 0.3 ms, more than 20 times faster than exact search.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Memory per vector in Faiss's HNSW is about 4 × d bytes for the vector plus about m × 2 × 4 bytes for layer-0 links. For d = 1,024 and m = 16 that is 4,096 + 128 = 4,224 bytes, so 10 million vectors need about 42 GB of RAM. That is why the compression lesson matters.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>HNSW is the standard ANN index in pgvector, Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Elasticsearch, OpenSearch and Redis, usually the default or the recommended choice. For billion-scale collections on SSD, teams use DiskANN-style indexes; on GPUs, IVF variants in Faiss or NVIDIA cuVS.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Building is slow and memory-heavy: tens of seconds for 100,000 vectors on a laptop, hours for hundreds of millions. Bulk-load first, then build the index.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Deletes and updates usually leave tombstones that degrade the graph; plan periodic rebuilds or compaction. Faiss's HNSW cannot remove vectors at all.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Recall drops silently as data grows or changes. Re-run a sample of queries with exact search now and then and track ANN recall.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Filters interact badly with graph search; that has its own lesson below.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you compare exact and HNSW search on 100,000 vectors for speed and recall?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build an exact (flat) index as ground truth and an HNSW index over the same 100,000 vectors, then run 1,000 held-out queries through both. For each query, recall@10 is the overlap between the HNSW top 10 and the exact top 10, divided by 10; average it, and measure per-query latency single-threaded. Repeat for several \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> values (for example 10, 16, 32, 64, 128) and plot recall against latency; expect HNSW to be 10 to 70 times faster with recall rising from about 0.8 to 0.99 or more as \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> grows, plus a build time of tens of seconds.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A query returns 9 of the true 10 nearest neighbours. What is its ANN recall@10, and which setting would you raise first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>0.9. Raise \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code>, which costs only query time and needs no rebuild; raise \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>ef_construction\u003c/code> (both require a rebuild) only if recall is still too low.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Roughly how much RAM does an HNSW index over 5 million 768-dimensional float vectors with m = 16 need?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About (4 × 768 + 16 × 2 × 4) = 3,072 + 128 = 3,200 bytes per vector, so 5,000,000 × 3,200 = 16 GB, plus overhead.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:vector-databases-pgvector-qdrant-chroma":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>vector database\u003c/b> stores vectors next to their text and metadata, keeps an ANN index up to date as you insert, update and delete, and answers \"nearest k, subject to these filters\" queries. If you already run Postgres, the \u003cb>pgvector\u003c/b> extension is usually the right first choice; dedicated engines such as \u003cb>Qdrant\u003c/b>, \u003cb>Weaviate\u003c/b> or \u003cb>Milvus\u003c/b> earn their place with very large collections, heavy filtering, high query rates or advanced compression.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What a database adds to an ANN library\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An ANN library such as Faiss or hnswlib gives you an in-memory index and nothing else. A vector database adds what production needs:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Persistence and updates:\u003c/b> upserts, deletes, crash recovery, backups.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Payloads (metadata):\u003c/b> document id, tenant, permissions, dates, stored and indexed for filtering.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Filtered search:\u003c/b> \"nearest 10 where tenant = 42 and language = 'de'\" (next-but-one lesson).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hybrid search:\u003c/b> keyword or sparse vectors next to dense vectors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scale and operations:\u003c/b> sharding, replication, quantisation, monitoring, access control.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The main options\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical fit\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>pgvector\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Postgres extension\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SQL, joins, transactions, row-level security, one system to run\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You already use Postgres; up to tens of millions of vectors\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Qdrant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-source engine in Rust, plus managed cloud\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast filtered search, quantisation, hybrid queries, multi-tenancy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dedicated vector workloads with heavy filtering\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weaviate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-source engine, plus managed cloud\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Built-in hybrid search and model integrations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teams wanting batteries included\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Milvus (Zilliz)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-source distributed engine\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Very large collections, GPU indexes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hundreds of millions to billions of vectors\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pinecone\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fully managed, serverless\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No operations at all\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teams that want zero infrastructure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Chroma, LanceDB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Embedded libraries (Chroma also runs as a server)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simplest start; LanceDB stores data as files, including object storage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prototypes, notebooks, local apps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Elasticsearch, OpenSearch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Search engines with vector fields\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mature BM25, aggregations, hybrid in one query\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Organisations that already run them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>You will also meet Vespa, turbopuffer, Redis, MongoDB Atlas Vector Search and cloud services such as Azure AI Search and Google Vertex AI Vector Search. The concepts in this module transfer to all of them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>pgvector in five statements\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">sql\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\nCREATE TABLE chunks (\n  id        bigserial PRIMARY KEY,\n  doc_id    text NOT NULL,\n  tenant_id int  NOT NULL,\n  content   text NOT NULL,\n  model     text NOT NULL,            -- embedding model and version\n  embedding vector(1024) NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n\nSET hnsw.ef_search = 100;             -- default is 40\n\nSELECT id, doc_id, 1 - (embedding &lt;=&gt; $1) AS cosine_similarity\nFROM chunks\nWHERE tenant_id = 42\nORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1             -- $1 is the query vector, e.g. '[0.12,-0.03,...]'\nLIMIT 5;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>pgvector's distance operators are \u003ccode>&lt;-&gt;\u003c/code> (L2 distance), \u003ccode>&lt;#&gt;\u003c/code> (negative inner product, because Postgres index scans only sort ascending), \u003ccode>&lt;=&gt;\u003c/code> (cosine distance, which is 1 minus cosine similarity) and \u003ccode>&lt;+&gt;\u003c/code> (L1 distance). The index operator class must match the operator you query with: \u003ccode>vector_cosine_ops\u003c/code> serves \u003ccode>&lt;=&gt;\u003c/code>. HNSW indexes on the \u003ccode>vector\u003c/code> type support up to 2,000 dimensions, and up to 4,000 with the half-precision \u003ccode>halfvec\u003c/code> type. The big practical advantage is that the vector lives in the same row and transaction as the text and metadata, so there is no second system to keep in sync.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The same thing in Qdrant\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from qdrant_client import QdrantClient, models\n\nclient = QdrantClient(url=\"http://localhost:6333\")\nclient.create_collection(\n    collection_name=\"chunks\",\n    vectors_config=models.VectorParams(size=1024, distance=models.Distance.COSINE),\n)\nclient.upsert(collection_name=\"chunks\", points=[\n    models.PointStruct(id=1, vector=vec, payload={\"doc_id\": \"kb-17\", \"tenant_id\": 42, \"content\": \"...\"}),\n])\nhits = client.query_points(\n    collection_name=\"chunks\",\n    query=query_vec,\n    query_filter=models.Filter(must=[models.FieldCondition(key=\"tenant_id\", match=models.MatchValue(value=42))]),\n    limit=5,\n).points\nfor h in hits:\n    print(h.id, round(h.score, 3), h.payload[\"doc_id\"])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The vocabulary differs (collections, points and payloads instead of tables, rows and columns), but the operations are the same.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How to choose\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Already on Postgres, under tens of millions of vectors?\u003c/b> Start with pgvector. You get joins with your application data, transactions, backups you already run, and row-level security.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Need very high query rates, billions of vectors, aggressive quantisation, or complex filtered and hybrid queries?\u003c/b> Benchmark a dedicated engine on your data and filters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prototyping?\u003c/b> Chroma or LanceDB in-process; move later behind the same interface.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No one to run infrastructure?\u003c/b> A managed service; check data residency and cost at your projected volume.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Whatever you choose, hide it behind a small interface in your code (\u003ccode>upsert(chunks)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>search(vector, k, filters)\u003c/code>), as you did for model clients. Migrations between vector stores are common.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-warn\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Watch out\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two pgvector surprises catch many teams. First, building an HNSW index on millions of rows needs memory; raise \u003ccode>maintenance_work_mem\u003c/code> and build after bulk loading. Second, with an approximate index the \u003ccode>WHERE\u003c/code> clause is applied \u003ci>after\u003c/i> the index scan, so a selective filter can return fewer than \u003ccode>LIMIT\u003c/code> rows; the filtering lesson explains the fixes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you store and query the same vectors in pgvector and one vector database, and what should you compare?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Embed the corpus once and save the vectors (for example as a NumPy file) so both systems get identical inputs. Load them into a Postgres table with a \u003ccode>vector(d)\u003c/code> column and an HNSW \u003ccode>vector_cosine_ops\u003c/code> index, and into a Qdrant collection with cosine distance and the same metadata as payload. Run the same 50 queries for the top 10 in both, check that the result sets overlap almost completely (small differences come from approximate search), then compare latency at your expected load, filtered-query behaviour, memory use and the operational effort of each.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>&lt;=&gt;\u003c/code> return in pgvector, and how do you turn it into cosine similarity?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It returns cosine distance, which is 1 minus cosine similarity, so similarity = 1 - (embedding &lt;=&gt; query).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two reasons to keep vectors in Postgres rather than a separate vector database.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: one system to run, back up and secure; vectors, text and metadata are updated in the same transaction so they never drift apart; you can join search results with application tables; Postgres row-level security can enforce tenant isolation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:keyword-search-and-bm25":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>BM25\u003c/b> is the classic keyword-ranking formula behind Elasticsearch, OpenSearch and most search engines. It rewards documents that contain the query's words, gives \u003cb>rare\u003c/b> words much more weight than common ones, lets repeated words help only up to a point, and penalises long documents. It beats embeddings on exact identifiers, codes, names and jargon, which is why serious retrieval systems use both.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How keyword search works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At indexing time, each document is \u003cb>tokenised\u003c/b> into terms (lowercased, punctuation stripped, often \u003cb>stemmed\u003c/b> so \"refunds\" and \"refunded\" become \"refund\") and stored in an \u003cb>inverted index\u003c/b>: for every term, the list of documents containing it and how often. At query time the engine looks up only the query's terms, so search is fast even over billions of documents. Then a scoring function ranks the matches; BM25 is the standard one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The BM25 formula, one idea at a time\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>score(D, Q) = Σ over query terms t:  IDF(t) × tf × (k1 + 1) / (tf + k1 × (1 - b + b × |D| / avgdl))\n\nIDF(t) = ln(1 + (N - n(t) + 0.5) / (n(t) + 0.5))       (the Lucene / Elasticsearch form)\n\ntf    = how often t occurs in document D      N     = number of documents\n|D|   = length of D in terms                  n(t)  = documents containing t\navgdl = average document length               k1 = 1.2, b = 0.75 (Elasticsearch defaults)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three ideas are packed in:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rare terms matter more (IDF).\u003c/b> A term in 3 of 1,000 documents is far more informative than one in 400 of them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Repetition saturates (k1).\u003c/b> The term-frequency part rises with tf but levels off towards k1 + 1 = 2.2. For a document of average length it is 1.0 at tf = 1, 1.375 at tf = 2, 1.57 at tf = 3, 1.77 at tf = 5 and 1.96 at tf = 10. Stuffing a word 50 times does not win.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Long documents are discounted (b).\u003c/b> With b = 0.75, a document twice the average length needs more occurrences for the same score. b = 0 turns length normalisation off.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>A worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>1,000 support chunks with an average length of 100 terms. The query is \"error E1234 timeout\". The term \"error\" appears in 400 chunks, \"E1234\" in 3 and \"timeout\" in 50.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>IDF(error)   = ln(1 + 600.5 / 400.5) = 0.916\nIDF(E1234)   = ln(1 + 997.5 / 3.5)   = 5.656\nIDF(timeout) = ln(1 + 950.5 / 50.5)  = 2.987\n\nChunk X (80 terms): error ×2, E1234 ×1, timeout ×0\n  error: 0.916 × (2 × 2.2) / (2 + 1.2 × (0.25 + 0.75 × 0.8))  = 1.335\n  E1234: 5.656 × (1 × 2.2) / (1 + 1.02)                       = 6.160\n  total                                                         7.495\n\nChunk Y (150 terms): error ×5, E1234 ×0, timeout ×3\n  error:   0.916 × (5 × 2.2) / (5 + 1.2 × (0.25 + 0.75 × 1.5)) = 1.515\n  timeout: 2.987 × (3 × 2.2) / (3 + 1.65)                       = 4.239\n  total                                                           5.755\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Chunk X wins because it contains the rare identifier, even though Y mentions errors and timeouts more often. A vector search would probably prefer Y, which is generically about timeout errors, and miss the one chunk about E1234. Anthropic's contextual retrieval post uses exactly this kind of example (\"Error code TS-999\") to explain why it pairs embeddings with BM25.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where each method wins\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>BM25 wins\u003c/th>\u003cth>Vectors win\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exact identifiers: error codes, SKUs, ticket and order numbers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Paraphrases and synonyms: \"money back\" for \"refund\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Function, class and API names in code and docs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Natural-language questions with few shared words\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rare names, acronyms and new jargon the embedding model never saw\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cross-lingual search with multilingual models\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exact phrases and quoted text\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vague or conceptual queries (\"why is my app slow?\")\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Out-of-domain text where embeddings were not trained\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short documents with little word overlap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The 2021 BEIR benchmark tested retrieval models on 18 datasets they were not trained on and found BM25 a robust baseline, with the dense models of the time often underperforming it out of domain. Modern embedding models have closed much of that gap, but the identifier and jargon cases above remain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Tools\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Search engines:\u003c/b> Elasticsearch and OpenSearch (BM25 is the default similarity), Vespa, and Tantivy (a Rust library).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Postgres:\u003c/b> built-in full-text search (\u003ccode>tsvector\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ts_rank\u003c/code>) gives stemming and ranking, though \u003ccode>ts_rank\u003c/code> is not BM25; extensions such as ParadeDB's \u003ccode>pg_search\u003c/code> add BM25 ranking.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>SQLite:\u003c/b> the FTS5 extension has a built-in \u003ccode>bm25()\u003c/code> ranking function.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python, in memory:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>rank_bm25\u003c/code> or the faster \u003ccode>bm25s\u003c/code> for prototypes and evals.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vector databases:\u003c/b> Qdrant, Weaviate and Pinecone offer BM25 or sparse vectors next to dense ones. \u003cb>Learned sparse\u003c/b> models such as SPLADE produce keyword-like sparse vectors with expansion terms, a middle ground.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from rank_bm25 import BM25Okapi\n\nchunks = [\"Error E1234: sync timeout after 30 s\", \"How to fix timeout errors in the sync client\",\n          \"Refund policy for EU customers\"]\ntokenise = lambda s: s.lower().replace(\":\", \" \").split()\nbm25 = BM25Okapi([tokenise(c) for c in chunks])\nprint(bm25.get_scores(tokenise(\"E1234 timeout\")))  # the first chunk scores highest\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Libraries differ slightly in their IDF formula and tokenisation, so compare rankings, not raw scores, across tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you run BM25 on the same queries as your vector search and find where it beats vectors?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Index the same chunks with BM25 (for example \u003ccode>bm25s\u003c/code> or Elasticsearch, with the same chunk ids), run your labelled query set through both retrievers, and compute recall@10 per query for each. List the queries where BM25 finds a relevant chunk that vector search misses; they are typically queries containing error codes, product or function names, ticket numbers, rare proper nouns, acronyms or exact phrases. The reverse list usually holds paraphrased or conversational questions, which is the evidence for combining both.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does BM25 give \"E1234\" far more weight than \"error\" in the example?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Because of IDF: \"E1234\" appears in 3 of 1,000 chunks, giving an IDF of 5.66, while \"error\" appears in 400, giving 0.92. Rare terms are much better evidence that a document is the one you want.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the k1 parameter stop?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It saturates term frequency, so each extra occurrence of a word adds less and less score (the term-frequency part never exceeds k1 + 1), which stops keyword-stuffed or very repetitive documents from dominating.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:hybrid-search-and-score-fusion":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Hybrid search\u003c/b> runs keyword (BM25) and vector search in parallel and merges the two ranked lists. \u003cb>Reciprocal rank fusion (RRF)\u003c/b> merges by rank, not score: each document gets 1 / (k + rank) from every list it appears in, and the sums decide the final order. It needs no score normalisation and no training, and it rewards documents that both retrievers agree on.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why fuse, and why not just add scores\u003c/h4>\u003cp>BM25 and vectors fail on different queries, as the previous lesson showed, so combining them raises recall. Anthropic's contextual retrieval experiments found embeddings plus BM25 better than embeddings alone across all their datasets.\u003c/p>\u003cp>You cannot simply add the scores: a BM25 score of 7.5 and a cosine of 0.83 live on different scales, and BM25's scale changes with every query. Two families of fix exist: fuse by \u003cb>rank\u003c/b> (RRF) or \u003cb>normalise scores\u003c/b> and then weight them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reciprocal rank fusion\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>RRF(d) = Σ over result lists r:  1 / (k + rank_r(d))      rank starts at 1; absent from a list = 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The constant k damps the advantage of the very top ranks. Cormack and colleagues fixed k = 60 in their 2009 paper, found it near-optimal but not critical, and showed RRF beating more sophisticated fusion and learning-to-rank methods on TREC data. It remains the default in Elasticsearch (\u003ccode>rank_constant\u003c/code> defaults to 60).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two retrievers return their top 5 for the same query:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>BM25:    D3, D1, D7, D2, D9\nVectors: D1, D5, D3, D8, D2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Doc\u003c/th>\u003cth>BM25 rank\u003c/th>\u003cth>Vector rank\u003c/th>\u003cth>BM25 part\u003c/th>\u003cth>Vector part\u003c/th>\u003cth>RRF score (k = 60)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Fused rank\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/62 = 0.0161\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/61 = 0.0164\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0325\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/61 = 0.0164\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/63 = 0.0159\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0323\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/64 = 0.0156\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/65 = 0.0154\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0310\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>absent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/62 = 0.0161\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0161\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D7\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>absent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/63 = 0.0159\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0159\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>absent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/64 = 0.0156\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0156\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>D9\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>absent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1/65 = 0.0154\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.0154\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Look at D2: it was only 4th and 5th in the two lists, yet it beats D5, which was 2nd in one list. With k = 60 the gap between rank 1 and rank 5 is small (0.0164 against 0.0154), so \u003cb>appearing in both lists matters more than being near the top of one\u003c/b>. A smaller k shifts weight to top positions: with k = 1, D1 scores 1/3 + 1/2 = 0.833 and D2 only 1/5 + 1/6 = 0.367.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-note\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Note\u003c/p>\u003cp>Defaults differ between systems. Qdrant's documentation, as of late 2026, writes the formula as 1 / (k + r) with zero-based ranks and a default k of 2, which is the same as k = 1 in the one-based formula above. Check the constant before comparing results across tools.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The whole algorithm fits in a few lines:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def rrf(rankings, k=60):\n    scores = {}\n    for ranking in rankings:\n        for rank, doc_id in enumerate(ranking, start=1):\n            scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0.0) + 1.0 / (k + rank)\n    return sorted(scores.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1], reverse=True)\n\nprint(rrf([[\"D3\", \"D1\", \"D7\", \"D2\", \"D9\"], [\"D1\", \"D5\", \"D3\", \"D8\", \"D2\"]]))\n# [('D1', 0.0325...), ('D3', 0.0322...), ('D2', 0.0310...), ('D5', 0.0161...), ...]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Score-based fusion\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The alternative is to normalise each list's scores to 0 to 1 and take a weighted sum: fused = α × vector + (1 - α) × keyword. \u003cb>Min-max normalisation\u003c/b> maps the best score in a list to 1 and the worst to 0. It has a catch: BM25 scores [12.4, 9.1, 3.3] become [1, 0.64, 0], and cosines [0.83, 0.81, 0.62] become [1, 0.90, 0], so a tiny cosine gap is stretched to look as large as a big BM25 gap. Qdrant's \u003cb>DBSF\u003c/b> (distribution-based score fusion) instead normalises using the mean and three standard deviations of each list. Weaviate's hybrid search exposes the weight as \u003ccode>alpha\u003c/code>. Score fusion can beat RRF when retrievers' scores are well calibrated, but it needs tuning on an eval set; RRF is the robust default.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Query\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>BM25\u003c/b>\u003csmall>top 50\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>RRF\u003c/b>\u003csmall>fuse and dedupe\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Top 20\u003c/b>\u003csmall>to reranker or prompt\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Query\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Embed query\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Vector search\u003c/b>\u003csmall>top 50\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>RRF\u003c/b>\u003csmall>fuse and dedupe\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>Retrieve 30 to 100 candidates from each retriever, fuse, and pass the top 10 to 50 to a reranker or straight to the prompt (the RAG module covers reranking).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003cb>weighted RRF\u003c/b> (a weight per list) if one retriever is clearly better on your eval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Measure recall@k for BM25 alone, vectors alone and the fused list. Hybrid should match or beat the better single retriever; if it does not, look at the queries where fusion pushed a good result down.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Most stores do it natively: Elasticsearch and OpenSearch have RRF retrievers, Qdrant uses prefetch plus \u003ccode>rrf\u003c/code>, Weaviate has a hybrid query, and in Postgres you can join a pgvector query with a full-text query and compute RRF in SQL (the pgvector README links an example).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you combine BM25 and vector results with reciprocal rank fusion?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run both retrievers for the same query and take, say, the top 50 from each. For every document, add 1 / (60 + rank) for each list it appears in (ranks start at 1, absent lists add nothing), then sort by the sum and keep the top results. No score normalisation is needed because only ranks are used, and documents found by both retrievers rise to the top.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Using k = 60, a document is ranked 1st by vectors and absent from BM25; another is 3rd in both. Which ranks higher?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The first scores 1/61 = 0.0164. The second scores 1/63 + 1/63 = 0.0317. The document found by both retrievers ranks higher.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can't you add a BM25 score to a cosine similarity directly?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They are on different, incomparable scales: BM25 is unbounded and varies with the query and corpus statistics, while cosine lies between -1 and 1, so the sum would be dominated by BM25 in an arbitrary way.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:embeddings/metadata-filtering-and-multi-tenancy":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Real queries are never \"nearest 10 in everything\": they are nearest 10 \u003ci>for this customer, in documents this user may see, for product version 4\u003c/i>. How the filter is combined with the ANN index decides whether you get 10 good results, 2 results, or someone else's data. Enforce tenant and permission filters on the server from the user's identity, and use an engine strategy that filters \u003ci>during\u003c/i> the search.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What goes into metadata\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store with each chunk the fields you will filter or display on: \u003ccode>doc_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tenant_id\u003c/code>, allowed groups or users (an \u003cb>access control list\u003c/b>, ACL), source system, language, product and version, document type, created and updated dates. Filters serve three purposes:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Security:\u003c/b> tenant isolation and permissions. These are mandatory and must never depend on the model or the client.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scope:\u003c/b> the user's product version, region or language.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Freshness:\u003c/b> only documents updated after a date, or prefer current versions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Three ways to combine a filter with ANN search\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good when\u003c/th>\u003cth>Problem\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Post-filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>ANN search first, then drop results that fail the filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The filter matches most rows\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Selective filters leave too few results\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pre-filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Find matching rows first (B-tree or payload index), then exact search over them\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The filter matches few rows (thousands)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slow when many rows match\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Filter during search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The engine skips non-matching nodes while walking the graph, with extra links so the graph stays connected\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Almost always; engines choose automatically\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs engine support and indexed filter fields\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The post-filter problem in numbers: HNSW explores about \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> candidates. pgvector's README gives the example: if a condition matches 10% of rows and \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> is 40 (the default), only about 4 rows survive on average. If the filter matches 2%, you expect 40 × 0.02 = 0.8 results for a \"top 10\" query. Users see empty answers and nobody notices until they complain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The fixes, by engine:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Qdrant\u003c/b> estimates how many points match the filter (its \u003cb>cardinality\u003c/b>) from payload indexes. If few match, it scores them directly; otherwise it searches a \u003cb>filterable HNSW\u003c/b> graph that has extra edges for indexed payload fields. Create payload indexes \u003ci>before\u003c/i> ingesting, because those edges are built with the graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>pgvector\u003c/b> 0.8.0 and later has \u003cb>iterative index scans\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>SET hnsw.iterative_scan = strict_order;\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>relaxed_order\u003c/code>) that keep scanning the index until enough rows pass the filter, up to \u003ccode>hnsw.max_scan_tuples\u003c/code>. For very selective filters, a B-tree index on the filter column lets Postgres do an exact search over just the matching rows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, Weaviate, Milvus\u003c/b> also filter during search; check how each behaves with very selective filters on your data.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Multi-tenancy patterns\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>tenant\u003c/b> is a customer organisation whose data must be isolated from everyone else's.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Isolation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Overhead\u003c/th>\u003cth>Fits\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Index or collection per tenant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strong\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High per tenant (memory, management); Qdrant Cloud caps clusters at 1,000 collections by default\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A few large or regulated tenants\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Shared index with a tenant field and filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Logical, by filter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many small, similar tenants\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Partition or shard per tenant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strong, physical\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mixed sizes; Postgres list partitioning, Qdrant user-defined sharding\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tiered\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small tenants shared, large ones promoted to their own shard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SaaS with a long tail plus big customers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In Qdrant, the shared pattern uses a keyword payload index marked as the tenant field, which stores each tenant's points together:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>client.create_payload_index(\n    collection_name=\"chunks\",\n    field_name=\"tenant_id\",\n    field_schema=models.KeywordIndexParams(type=models.KeywordIndexType.KEYWORD, is_tenant=True),\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>pgvector's README warns that a shared approximate index lets one tenant's vectors affect recall and speed for others, and suggests list partitioning (\u003ccode>PARTITION BY LIST(customer_id)\u003c/code>) or separate tables when isolation matters.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Security rules\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Take \u003ccode>tenant_id\u003c/code> and the user's groups from the \u003cb>authenticated session\u003c/b> on the server. Never from a request parameter the client controls, and never from the model's tool-call arguments. A prompt injection must not be able to widen the filter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Apply permission filters \u003cb>inside\u003c/b> the retrieval query, not by hiding results afterwards; otherwise a \"top 10\" can contain nothing the user may see.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In Postgres, \u003cb>row-level security\u003c/b> policies add a second line of defence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write \u003cb>leak tests\u003c/b>: user A searches for a phrase that exists only in tenant B's documents and must get nothing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Deletion must reach every copy: vectors, BM25 index, caches and any contextualised text (the Advanced retrieval module covers keeping permissions and deletions in sync).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your filter matches 2% of 1 million chunks and a post-filtered HNSW query for the top 10 returns 0 or 1 results. Why, and what are two fixes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The index only explores about \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> candidates (40 by default in pgvector), and only 2% of them pass the filter, so you expect 40 × 0.02 = 0.8 results. Fixes: filter during the search (pgvector iterative index scans, Qdrant's filterable HNSW with a payload index); pre-filter with a B-tree index and run an exact search over the 20,000 matching rows; partition the data by the filter field; or, as a stopgap, raise \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the tenant filter come from the authenticated session rather than from the LLM's tool call?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model's arguments can be manipulated by prompt injection or simply be wrong, and the client can be tampered with; only the server-side identity is trustworthy, so it must set the filter that enforces isolation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you give each tenant its own collection instead of sharing one with a tenant filter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When there are few tenants that are large or regulated and need strong physical isolation, separate deletion or residency guarantees; with thousands of small tenants, per-tenant collections waste memory and hit management limits, so a shared collection with an indexed tenant field (or tiered sharding) fits better.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:embeddings/vector-compression-quantisation-and-rescoring":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>At scale, memory is the main cost of vector search, and float32 vectors are wasteful. \u003cb>Quantisation\u003c/b> stores each vector in fewer bits: int8 (4 times smaller), binary (32 times smaller) or product-quantised codes (64 times smaller or more). You search the small vectors to get candidates, then \u003cb>rescore\u003c/b> the best few with full-precision vectors, which recovers most of the lost quality.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Where the memory goes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>10 million chunks × 1,024 dimensions × 4 bytes (float32) = 40.96 GB of vectors\n+ HNSW links: about m × 2 × 4 = 128 bytes per vector     =  1.28 GB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>HNSW needs its vectors in RAM to be fast, so this is 40+ GB of RAM per replica before any metadata. RAM, not compute, usually decides the bill.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The techniques\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Technique\u003c/th>\u003cth>Bytes per 1,024-dim vector\u003c/th>\u003cth>10M vectors\u003c/th>\u003cth>Smaller by\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical quality kept\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>float32 (baseline)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4,096\u003c/td>\u003ctd>40.96 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>float16 (\u003ccode>halfvec\u003c/code> in pgvector)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,048\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20.48 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Practically all\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Matryoshka: keep 256 dims, float32\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.24 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model-dependent; small loss for models trained for it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scalar int8\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,024\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.24 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High, often 95% or more\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Binary (1 bit per dimension)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>128\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.28 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model-dependent; recovered largely by rescoring\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Product quantisation (64 codes of 1 byte)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.64 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>64x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lowest; needs rescoring\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Scalar (int8) quantisation\u003c/b> maps each dimension's typical range onto 256 integer levels, using ranges computed from a calibration sample of vectors. If a dimension ranges from -0.2 to 0.2, the value 0.05 becomes level round(0.625 × 255) = 159, stored as the int8 value 31; converting back gives 0.0494, an error of 0.0006.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Binary quantisation\u003c/b> keeps only the sign: 1 if the value is above 0, otherwise 0. The distance between two binary vectors is the \u003cb>Hamming distance\u003c/b>, the number of differing bits, computed with an XOR and a population count, one of the fastest operations a CPU has.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>values: [0.12, -0.40, 0.05, 0.33, -0.02, -0.18, 0.27, -0.09]  -&gt;  bits 10110010\nother vector's bits                                                    10100011\nXOR                                                                    00010001  -&gt;  Hamming distance 2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Product quantisation (PQ)\u003c/b> splits each vector into, say, 64 sub-vectors of 16 dimensions, runs k-means with 256 centroids in each sub-space, and stores only the centroid number (1 byte) per sub-vector: 64 bytes per vector. At query time a 64 × 256 table of query-to-centroid distances turns every distance into 64 table lookups. Faiss's IVF-PQ indexes combine this with inverted lists for billion-scale search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Rescoring and oversampling\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compression scrambles the fine ordering near the top, but the right answers are almost always somewhere in a slightly larger candidate set. So:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Query\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Compressed index\u003c/b>\u003csmall>fetch top 40 (k × 4 oversampling)\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Rescore\u003c/b>\u003csmall>full-precision vectors\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Top 10\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The full-precision vectors can sit on disk, because you only read 40 of them per query. The Hugging Face benchmark (retrieval tasks from MTEB, nDCG@10) shows how much this matters, and how model-dependent it is:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>int8 kept (no rescoring)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Binary with rescoring kept\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>mxbai-embed-large-v1 (1,024 dims)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>97%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>96.45%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384 dims)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>90.79%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>93.79%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>e5-base-v2 (768 dims)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>94.68%\u003c/td>\u003ctd>74.77%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The post reports that binary quantisation kept roughly 92.5% of retrieval performance without rescoring and up to about 96% with it, at 32 times less memory. But e5-base-v2 lost a quarter of its quality in binary form: always measure your own model. Qdrant's guide, as of late 2026, suggests starting with an oversampling factor of 3, rescoring on, quantised vectors in RAM and originals on disk. Some models are trained to survive quantisation, and some APIs (for example Cohere's) can return int8 or binary embeddings directly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In pgvector\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">sql\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>-- index the binary form, search it, then rescore the candidates with full vectors\nCREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw ((binary_quantize(embedding)::bit(1024)) bit_hamming_ops);\n\nSELECT id FROM (\n  SELECT id, embedding FROM chunks\n  ORDER BY binary_quantize(embedding)::bit(1024) &lt;~&gt; binary_quantize($1)\n  LIMIT 40\n) candidates\nORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1\nLIMIT 10;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>&lt;~&gt;\u003c/code> is pgvector's Hamming-distance operator. The simplest saving of all is the \u003ccode>halfvec\u003c/code> type, which halves memory with practically no quality loss.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Start with float32 or float16 while the corpus is small. Compression is an optimisation, not a starting point.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When RAM drives cost, try int8 first; then binary or PQ with oversampling and rescoring for larger savings. Combine with Matryoshka truncation if your model supports it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Measure recall@10 of each configuration against the uncompressed baseline on your labelled queries, plus latency and memory. Keep the configuration that stays within about a point or two of the baseline.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How much memory do 50 million 768-dimensional vectors need as float32, int8 and binary?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>float32: 50,000,000 × 768 × 4 bytes = 153.6 GB. int8: 50,000,000 × 768 bytes = 38.4 GB. Binary: 768 bits = 96 bytes per vector, so 50,000,000 × 96 = 4.8 GB (plus index overhead in each case).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does rescoring recover most of the quality lost by binary quantisation at little cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The compressed search reliably puts the true best results somewhere in a slightly larger candidate set (for example the top 40 for a top 10), and recomputing exact similarity for just those 40 vectors is cheap; only the fine ordering was lost, not the candidates.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must you test quantisation on your own embedding model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Models differ in how much information survives compression: in the Hugging Face benchmark one model kept 96% of its quality in binary form with rescoring while another kept only about 75%.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:self-hosted-embedding-inference":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Embedding models are small enough to run yourself, and an embedding server turns one into an HTTP API with batching. Teams self-host for \u003cb>privacy, control and latency\u003c/b> more often than for cost, because API embeddings are cheap. The work is choosing a server, sizing it, and measuring two different things: bulk \u003cb>throughput\u003c/b> for ingestion and single-query \u003cb>latency\u003c/b> for search.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why self-host, and why not\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reasons to run your own:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Data control:\u003c/b> documents never leave your network, which some contracts and regulators require.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stability:\u003c/b> you pin exact weights forever. When an API provider retires an embedding model you must re-embed the whole corpus; your own model never disappears.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Latency and limits:\u003c/b> a server next to your vector store avoids internet round trips and provider rate limits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Choice:\u003c/b> any open model, including domain fine-tunes and rerankers.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Reasons not to: you now operate GPUs or CPU fleets, monitoring and upgrades. And the cost argument is weak at modest volume. Embedding 100 million tokens through an API priced around $0.02 per million (the small OpenAI model, as of late 2026) costs about $2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The servers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Server\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Text Embeddings Inference (TEI)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hugging Face's Rust server for embedding, reranking and classification models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Token-based dynamic batching, Flash Attention, Prometheus metrics and OpenTelemetry tracing; CPU and GPU images\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>vLLM\u003c/td>\u003ctd>General LLM inference engine with a pooling runner\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Serves embedding models on \u003ccode>/v1/embeddings\u003c/code> and rerankers on \u003ccode>/rerank\u003c/code>; suits LLM-based embedders such as the Qwen3 series\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ollama\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Local model runner\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>/api/embed\u003c/code> returns L2-normalised vectors; OpenAI-compatible \u003ccode>/v1/embeddings\u003c/code>; good for laptops and small teams\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sentence-transformers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python library, in-process\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simplest for batch jobs and experiments; no server\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Starting TEI with a GPU (image tags change; check the quick tour for the current one, here \u003ccode>cuda-1.9\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>cpu-1.9\u003c/code> for CPU):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>model=Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B\ndocker run --gpus all -p 8080:80 -v $PWD/data:/data --pull always \\\n  ghcr.io/huggingface/text-embeddings-inference:cuda-1.9 --model-id $model\n\ncurl 127.0.0.1:8080/embed -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"inputs\": [\"What is deep learning?\", \"How do I reset my password?\"]}'\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>TEI also exposes \u003ccode>/v1/embeddings\u003c/code> in the OpenAI format, so the client code from the first lesson works unchanged with \u003ccode>base_url=\"http://localhost:8080/v1\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What drives throughput\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Model size:\u003c/b> a 0.6B-parameter embedder is several times slower than a 100M-parameter one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sequence length:\u003c/b> attention cost grows faster than linearly with tokens, so 512-token chunks cost much more than 128-token queries. TEI truncates over-long inputs by default (\u003ccode>--auto-truncate\u003c/code>), which is convenient but can silently drop the end of long chunks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Batching:\u003c/b> GPUs are efficient only with many texts per forward pass. TEI batches by token count across concurrent requests; tune \u003ccode>--max-batch-tokens\u003c/code> and send several requests at once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Precision and hardware:\u003c/b> float16 or bfloat16 on a modern GPU; ONNX or quantised models for CPU serving.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Measuring it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time, statistics, concurrent.futures as cf, requests\n\nURL = \"http://localhost:8080/embed\"\ntexts = [...]                                   # e.g. 10,000 real chunks from your corpus\nbatches = [texts[i:i + 32] for i in range(0, len(texts), 32)]\n\ndef send(batch):\n    r = requests.post(URL, json={\"inputs\": batch}, timeout=120)\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return len(batch)\n\nt = time.perf_counter()                         # 1. bulk throughput, 8 concurrent clients\nwith cf.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=8) as pool:\n    done = sum(pool.map(send, batches))\nprint(f\"{done / (time.perf_counter() - t):.0f} texts/s\")\n\nlat = []                                        # 2. single-query latency, one at a time\nfor q in texts[:200]:\n    t = time.perf_counter(); send([q[:300]]); lat.append((time.perf_counter() - t) * 1000)\nq = statistics.quantiles(lat, n=100)\nprint(f\"p50 {q[49]:.1f} ms, p95 {q[94]:.1f} ms\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Multiply texts per second by your average tokens per chunk (count them with the model's tokeniser) to get tokens per second. Then cost per million tokens on rented hardware is:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>cost per million tokens = GPU price per hour / (tokens per second × 3,600 / 1,000,000)\nillustrative: $1.50 per hour at 20,000 tokens/s = 72 million tokens/hour -&gt; about $0.021 per million\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>That only holds at full utilisation. An idle GPU still costs $1.50 an hour, which is why self-hosting pays off on cost only with high, steady volume.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ingestion and search have different needs. Ingestion is a throughput job: big batches, many concurrent requests, possibly a large temporary GPU fleet or an API's batch mode. Query embedding is a latency job: one short text, which a small model on a CPU can often handle in tens of milliseconds, but measure it. Whatever you serve, keep the model revision, pooling, normalisation and query prefix identical on both paths, and record them with the index.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you serve an open embedding model and measure its throughput?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run it in a server such as TEI (\u003ccode>docker run ... text-embeddings-inference --model-id &lt;model&gt;\u003c/code>), warm it up, then send a realistic workload: for example 10,000 real chunks in batches of 32 from 8 concurrent clients, timing the whole run to get texts per second and, using the average token count, tokens per second. Separately send 200 single short queries one at a time and report p50 and p95 latency. Repeat with different batch sizes and concurrency, compare GPU against CPU, and convert throughput into cost per million tokens at the hardware's hourly price.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A GPU costs $2 per hour and embeds 10,000 tokens per second. What is the cost per million tokens at full utilisation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>10,000 × 3,600 = 36 million tokens per hour, so $2 / 36 = about $0.056 per million tokens, and more whenever the GPU sits idle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two reasons to self-host embeddings that have nothing to do with price.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: documents must not leave your network; you can pin the exact model forever and never be forced to re-embed by a deprecation; lower and more predictable latency without rate limits; freedom to use domain fine-tuned models and rerankers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:multimodal-embeddings":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>multimodal embedding model\u003c/b> puts images and text into the \u003ci>same\u003c/i> vector space, so a text query can find images (and an image can find text) with ordinary nearest-neighbour search. CLIP made this mainstream by training two encoders contrastively on image-caption pairs. It is powerful for photos and products, weaker for reading text inside images, and its cross-modal scores behave differently from text-to-text scores.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How CLIP-style training works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>CLIP (OpenAI, 2021) has an \u003cb>image encoder\u003c/b> and a \u003cb>text encoder\u003c/b>, each producing a vector of the same size. Training takes a batch of N image-caption pairs, embeds all of them, and computes the N × N matrix of image-to-caption similarities. The loss pushes the N matching pairs on the diagonal up and every mismatched pair down.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>              image 1   image 2   image 3   image 4\ncaption 1       high      low       low       low     maximise the diagonal,\ncaption 2       low       high      low       low     minimise everything else\ncaption 3       low       low       high      low\ncaption 4       low       low       low       high\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Trained this way on 400 million image-text pairs from the internet, CLIP could classify images it was never trained to classify: embed the labels as text (\"a photo of a dog\", \"a photo of a cat\") and pick the label closest to the image. Zero-shot, it matched the accuracy of the original ResNet-50 on ImageNet without using any of the 1.28 million ImageNet training examples. SigLIP (Google, 2023) replaced the batch-wide softmax with a simple per-pair sigmoid loss, which trains well at smaller batch sizes; SigLIP-family encoders are now common in open vision-language models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Text-to-image search in a few lines\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from PIL import Image\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"clip-ViT-B-32\")\npaths = [\"img/001.jpg\", \"img/002.jpg\", \"img/003.jpg\"]           # your images\nimg = model.encode([Image.open(p) for p in paths], normalize_embeddings=True)\ntxt = model.encode([\"a red bicycle leaning on a wall\"], normalize_embeddings=True)\nscores = (txt @ img.T)[0]                                        # cosine similarities\nprint([paths[i] for i in scores.argsort()[::-1][:5]])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In production you store the image vectors in your vector database exactly like text vectors, embed each text query with the text encoder, and search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Uses\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Text-to-image search in photo libraries, product catalogues and media archives.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Image-to-image search: near-duplicate detection, \"more like this\", visual product search.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Zero-shot image classification and tagging from a list of label texts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Content moderation and filtering against textual descriptions of disallowed content.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Video search by embedding sampled frames.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Documents, screenshots and the newer models\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Business documents are mostly text in images: scanned pages, slides, charts, tables. Classic CLIP models read text in images poorly, and their text encoder accepts only short inputs (77 tokens), so they are a bad fit. Two patterns work better:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Describe, then embed as text.\u003c/b> A vision-language model writes a caption or extracts the content of each image or page; you embed that text with your normal text model. Everything stays in one text index, BM25 works on the captions, and citations are readable. The cost is one model call per image at ingestion.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Document-aware multimodal embeddings.\u003c/b> As of late 2026, commercial models such as Voyage's multimodal embeddings (which take interleaved text and images, including screenshots of PDFs, slides and tables) and Cohere's Embed models accept images directly, and open late-interaction models such as ColPali embed page screenshots. The Advanced retrieval module covers ColPali and multimodal RAG.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The modality gap.\u003c/b> In CLIP-like models, images and texts occupy separate regions of the shared space (Liang et al., 2022, \"Mind the Gap\"). Text-to-image similarities are systematically lower than text-to-text ones. In a mixed index, a text query tends to rank text items above images, and one similarity threshold cannot serve both. Keep separate indexes per modality and fuse with RRF, or caption the images.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Weak spots:\u003c/b> counting objects, spatial relations (\"left of\"), negation, fine-grained distinctions, and text inside images.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompt wording matters:\u003c/b> \"a photo of a {label}\" often works better than the bare label for zero-shot classification.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate\u003c/b> with labelled text queries and their correct images, using the same recall@k metrics as text search.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you search images with text using a CLIP-style model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Embed every image once with the model's image encoder, L2-normalise, and store the vectors with their file paths or ids in a vector index. At query time, embed the text with the same model's text encoder, normalise, and return the images with the highest cosine similarity. Check quality with a set of labelled text queries and their correct images, measuring recall@k.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does CLIP classify images zero-shot?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Because labels can be written as text and embedded into the same space as images; the label whose text vector is closest to the image vector is the prediction, so no task-specific training is needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your mixed index of help articles and screenshots always ranks articles above screenshots for text queries. What is going on and what can you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The modality gap: text-to-image similarities are systematically lower than text-to-text ones, so text items win. Search separate indexes per modality and fuse the rankings with RRF, or caption the screenshots with a vision model and index the captions as text.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:embeddings/measuring-retrieval-recall-mrr-and-ndcg":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Retrieval can be measured cheaply and deterministically, without an LLM judge: label which documents answer each test query, then score the ranked results. \u003cb>Recall@k\u003c/b> asks \"did the right chunks make it into the top k?\", \u003cb>MRR\u003c/b> asks \"how high was the first right one?\", and \u003cb>nDCG\u003c/b> rewards putting the most relevant results first. For RAG, recall@k at the number of chunks you send to the model is the metric to watch.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The labelled query set\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You need a list of queries, each with its \u003cb>relevant\u003c/b> documents or chunks (in information-retrieval jargon, \u003cb>qrels\u003c/b>). Ways to build one:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Real queries\u003c/b> from logs or support tickets, labelled by someone who knows the content. Best, because they reflect real wording.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Synthetic queries:\u003c/b> sample chunks and ask an LLM to write a question each one answers, in the words a user would use rather than copying the text. Review and discard poor ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pooling for labels:\u003c/b> run several retrievers, take the union of their top 10, and have a human (or a validated LLM judge) mark each candidate relevant or not.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Start with 50 queries, grow to 100 to 200, and tag each by type (exact identifier, how-to question, paraphrase, multi-part) so you can see where a system fails. Label at the \u003cb>document or answer-span\u003c/b> level when you can: chunk ids change every time you re-chunk, and you will want to compare chunking strategies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The metrics\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\u003cth>Definition\u003c/th>\u003cth>Answers\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>precision@k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>relevant results in the top k / k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How much of what I return is useful (noise in the prompt)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>recall@k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>relevant results in the top k / all relevant results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Did I find what is needed?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>hit rate@k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 if any relevant result is in the top k, else 0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>recall@k when one result is enough\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>reciprocal rank (RR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 / rank of the first relevant result (0 if none)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How quickly does the user hit a good result?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MRR\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mean of RR over all queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Average version of the above\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>nDCG@k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DCG / ideal DCG, where DCG = Σ gain / log2(rank + 1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Are the most relevant results ranked highest?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A query has three relevant chunks, {D2, D1, D5}. The system returns, in order: D4, D2, D9, D1, D7.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>precision@5 = 2 relevant in top 5 / 5     = 0.40\nrecall@5    = 2 found / 3 relevant        = 0.67\nRR          = first relevant at rank 2    = 1/2 = 0.50\n\nnDCG@5 with binary gains (1 = relevant, 0 = not):\ndiscounts 1 / log2(rank + 1): rank1 1.000, rank2 0.631, rank3 0.500, rank4 0.431, rank5 0.387\nDCG   = 0 + 1×0.631 + 0 + 1×0.431 + 0  = 1.062\nIDCG  = best possible order, 3 relevant at ranks 1 to 3 = 1.000 + 0.631 + 0.500 = 2.131\nnDCG@5 = 1.062 / 2.131 = 0.498\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>MRR across queries.\u003c/b> If four queries have reciprocal ranks 0.5, 1, 0.333 and 0 (nothing relevant found), MRR = (0.5 + 1 + 0.333 + 0) / 4 = 0.458.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Graded relevance.\u003c/b> nDCG shines when relevance has levels. Say D2 is a perfect answer (grade 3), D5 good (2) and D1 marginal (1). With gain equal to the grade: DCG = 3 × 0.631 + 1 × 0.431 = 2.324; ideal order D2, D5, D1 gives IDCG = 3 × 1 + 2 × 0.631 + 1 × 0.5 = 4.762; nDCG@5 = 0.488. Some tools use the gain 2^grade - 1 instead, which rewards top grades more; state which you use. MTEB's main retrieval metric is nDCG@10.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\n\ndef recall_at_k(ranked, relevant, k):\n    return len(set(ranked[:k]) &amp; relevant) / len(relevant)\n\ndef reciprocal_rank(ranked, relevant):\n    return next((1 / i for i, d in enumerate(ranked, start=1) if d in relevant), 0.0)\n\ndef ndcg_at_k(ranked, gains, k):                 # gains: {doc_id: grade}\n    dcg = sum(gains.get(d, 0) / math.log2(i + 1) for i, d in enumerate(ranked[:k], start=1))\n    ideal = sorted(gains.values(), reverse=True)[:k]\n    idcg = sum(g / math.log2(i + 1) for i, g in enumerate(ideal, start=1))\n    return dcg / idcg if idcg else 0.0\n\nranked = [\"D4\", \"D2\", \"D9\", \"D1\", \"D7\"]\nprint(recall_at_k(ranked, {\"D2\", \"D1\", \"D5\"}, 5), reciprocal_rank(ranked, {\"D2\", \"D1\", \"D5\"}),\n      ndcg_at_k(ranked, {\"D2\": 1, \"D1\": 1, \"D5\": 1}, 5))   # 0.666..., 0.5, 0.498...\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Which metric when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>RAG:\u003c/b> recall@k where k is the number of chunks you pass to the model (or into the reranker). A chunk ranked 4th or 9th is equally useful if both are in the prompt; one ranked 21st is useless.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Search boxes and navigation:\u003c/b> MRR or nDCG@10, because users look at the top few results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Context noise:\u003c/b> precision@k shows how many irrelevant chunks you pay for and distract the model with.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ranking quality with graded labels:\u003c/b> nDCG.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Reading the numbers honestly\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Report averages \u003cb>and\u003c/b> per-slice numbers: hybrid search may lift identifier queries from 0.4 to 0.9 recall while barely moving paraphrases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With 50 queries, one query moves recall by 2 points, so differences of a few points are often noise. Compare systems on the same queries (paired), look at which queries changed, and grow the set before trusting small gains. The Evals module covers confidence intervals.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Always read the failures. A \"miss\" is sometimes a missing label rather than a bad result; fix the label and note it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A query has 4 relevant chunks; the top 10 results contain 3 of them, the first at rank 3. What are recall@10, precision@10 and the reciprocal rank?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>recall@10 = 3 / 4 = 0.75; precision@10 = 3 / 10 = 0.3; reciprocal rank = 1 / 3 = 0.333.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is recall@k usually more useful than MRR for a RAG system?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model reads all k retrieved chunks, so what matters is whether the needed chunks are anywhere in the top k, not whether one is at rank 1; MRR mostly measures the position of the first hit, which matters for humans scanning a results list.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why label relevance at document or answer-span level rather than by chunk id?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Chunk ids change whenever you change the chunking strategy, size or parser, which would invalidate your labels; document ids or answer text spans let you score any chunking against the same query set.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:embeddings/exact-versus-hnsw-on-100-000-vectors":"\u003cp>You will build an exact index and an HNSW index over the same 100,000 vectors, measure recall@10 and latency across \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> values, and see the trade-off from the HNSW lesson with your own numbers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10+, \u003ccode>pip install faiss-cpu numpy\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>About 1 GB of free RAM; no GPU needed\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional, for real embeddings: \u003ccode>pip install sentence-transformers\u003c/code> and a text dataset of 100,000 short passages\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>hnsw_lab.py\u003c/code>. It makes clustered unit vectors as a stand-in for real embeddings, so the lab runs in a minute.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nimport faiss\nimport numpy as np\n\nN, D, Q, K = 100_000, 384, 500, 10\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n\ndef make(n, centres):\n    \"\"\"Clustered unit vectors: a stand-in for real embeddings.\"\"\"\n    x = centres[rng.integers(0, len(centres), n)] + 1.2 * rng.standard_normal((n, D)).astype(\"float32\")\n    return (x / np.linalg.norm(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)).astype(\"float32\")\n\ncentres = rng.standard_normal((1_000, D)).astype(\"float32\")\nxb, xq = make(N, centres), make(Q, centres)\n\ndef timed_search(index, queries, k):\n    \"\"\"Search one query at a time, like a live service; return ids and ms per query.\"\"\"\n    index.search(queries[:20], k)                     # warm-up\n    ids, t = [], time.perf_counter()\n    for q in queries:\n        ids.append(index.search(q[None, :], k)[1][0])\n    return np.array(ids), (time.perf_counter() - t) / len(queries) * 1000\n\nfaiss.omp_set_num_threads(1)\nexact = faiss.IndexFlatIP(D)                          # inner product = cosine on unit vectors\nexact.add(xb)\ntruth, exact_ms = timed_search(exact, xq, K)\nprint(f\"exact: {exact_ms:.2f} ms/query\")\n\nfaiss.omp_set_num_threads(8)                          # build with several threads\nhnsw = faiss.IndexHNSWFlat(D, 16, faiss.METRIC_INNER_PRODUCT)   # m = 16\nhnsw.hnsw.efConstruction = 100\nt = time.perf_counter()\nhnsw.add(xb)\nprint(f\"HNSW build: {time.perf_counter() - t:.1f} s, index {faiss.serialize_index(hnsw).nbytes / 1e6:.0f} MB \"\n      f\"(raw vectors {xb.nbytes / 1e6:.0f} MB)\")\n\nfaiss.omp_set_num_threads(1)                          # query single-threaded for fair timing\nfor ef in (10, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256):\n    hnsw.hnsw.efSearch = ef\n    got, ms = timed_search(hnsw, xq, K)\n    recall = np.mean([len(set(g) &amp; set(t)) / K for g, t in zip(got, truth)])\n    print(f\"ef_search={ef:&gt;3}  recall@10={recall:.3f}  {ms:.3f} ms/query  {exact_ms / ms:.0f}x faster\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Run it with \u003ccode>python hnsw_lab.py\u003c/code> and copy the output into a table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Plot recall@10 (y axis) against milliseconds per query (x axis), one point per \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code>. Mark the cheapest point with recall of at least 0.95 and at least 0.99.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> from 16 to 8 and then 32. Re-run and note build time, index size and the recall at \u003ccode>ef_search = 16\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make the problem harder: raise the noise factor from 1.2 to 2.0 in \u003ccode>make\u003c/code> (clusters overlap more, like real embeddings of a diverse corpus) and re-run. Note how much higher \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> must go for 0.99 recall.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional: replace \u003ccode>xb\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>xq\u003c/code> with real embeddings (for example 100,000 passages embedded with a small sentence-transformers model, normalised), keeping 500 passages aside as queries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On a recent laptop or server CPU, with the default settings:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Exact search takes around 10 ms per query, single-threaded, and is always perfectly accurate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The HNSW build takes tens of seconds, and the index is only about 10% larger than the raw vectors (the graph links).\u003c/li>\u003cli>At \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> 10 to 16, recall@10 is around 0.8 to 0.93 at roughly 0.1 to 0.2 ms per query, 50 or more times faster than exact. Around 32, recall reaches about 0.99 while still 20 or more times faster. Beyond that, recall hardly improves while latency keeps doubling.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Smaller \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> builds faster and uses less memory but needs a larger \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> for the same recall; larger \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> does the opposite.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Harder data needs a much larger \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> for the same recall. Real embeddings usually sit between the easy and hard synthetic settings, which is why you tune on your own data.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which \u003ccode>ef_search\u003c/code> would you choose for a RAG system that retrieves the top 10, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The smallest value that gives recall@10 of about 0.98 to 0.99 on your data (around 32 in this lab), because higher values add latency with almost no recall gain, while lower ones silently drop relevant chunks that the language model then never sees.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:semantic-search-engine":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M6. It has a provider-agnostic client with cost logging, a prompt registry with regression tests, typed tools, a context assembler and memory, but it still knows nothing about your document collection. M7 adds the retrieval layer: ingest the corpus, index it for both keyword and vector search, fuse the two with reciprocal rank fusion, and measure it. M8 (in the RAG module) will turn these search results into grounded, cited answers, so build search as a clean, separately testable component.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A corpus of 200 to 2,000 documents you care about (team docs, a help centre, or public docs such as Python or Kubernetes), converted to Markdown or plain text with \u003ccode>doc_id\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>title\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>url\u003c/code> and an access field such as \u003ccode>tenant_id\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>groups\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Simple chunking at headings and paragraphs, 200 to 500 tokens per chunk, with the document title and section path prepended to the chunk text. (M8 upgrades parsing and chunking.)\u003c/li>\u003cli>An \u003ccode>atlas/embed.py\u003c/code> interface over one API model and one open model, with batching and retries; store the model name and version with every vector, and log embedding cost through the M2 meter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One store: Postgres with pgvector (HNSW, cosine) plus a keyword index (Postgres full-text, ParadeDB \u003ccode>pg_search\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>bm25s\u003c/code> in memory), or Qdrant with dense vectors plus BM25.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>search(query, k=10, filters=...)\u003c/code> with three modes, \u003ccode>bm25\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vector\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>hybrid\u003c/code> (RRF, k = 60, 50 candidates per retriever), returning chunk ids, text, per-retriever ranks and the fused score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tenant or permission filters applied inside the query from the caller's identity, never from model output.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>eval/retrieval.jsonl\u003c/code> with 50 labelled queries: about 15 with exact identifiers or rare terms, 20 natural questions and 15 paraphrases, each with relevant document ids and a type tag.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An eval script reporting recall@10, MRR@10 and nDCG@10 for each mode, overall and per type, plus p50 and p95 search latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>search_docs\u003c/code> registered as a typed tool in the M4 tool registry.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Write the ingestion script: load, clean, chunk, assign stable chunk ids (\u003ccode>doc_id\u003c/code> plus position). Re-running it must not create duplicates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Embed and load the vectors; build the HNSW index after the bulk load. Check a few nearest-neighbour results by eye.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add the keyword index and \u003ccode>bm25\u003c/code> mode. Try a query with an identifier and confirm it wins there.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement RRF and the \u003ccode>hybrid\u003c/code> mode, with unit tests using the worked example from the hybrid lesson.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write and label the 50 queries. Label by document id, and keep the labelling notes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the eval script and produce the table: three modes by three metrics, overall and per query type.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read every query where hybrid misses; fix bugs or labels, and write a short findings note with three concrete failure examples.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Register \u003ccode>search_docs\u003c/code> as a tool and ask Atlas a few questions that make it call the tool.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python -m atlas.eval.retrieval\u003c/code> rebuilds nothing and prints the metrics table in under a minute.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Hybrid matches or beats the better single retriever on recall@10, or the findings note explains why not with examples.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A leak test passes: a query for text that exists only in another tenant's documents returns nothing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Re-running ingestion is idempotent, and changing the embedding model creates a new, separately named index.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare two embedding models, and full against Matryoshka-reduced dimensions, on the same eval.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add int8 or binary quantisation with rescoring and report the recall and memory change.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Serve the open embedding model with TEI and record its throughput and query latency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tune RRF: try k = 10 and k = 100, and weighted RRF, and keep what the eval supports.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reciprocal rank fusion in one Postgres query (a sketch to adapt; \u003ccode>tsv\u003c/code> is a \u003ccode>tsvector\u003c/code> column, \u003ccode>$1\u003c/code> the query vector and \u003ccode>$2\u003c/code> the query text):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">sql\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>WITH semantic AS (\n  SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1) AS rank\n  FROM chunks WHERE tenant_id = $3\n  ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 50\n), keyword AS (\n  SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ts_rank_cd(tsv, q) DESC) AS rank\n  FROM chunks, plainto_tsquery('english', $2) q\n  WHERE tsv @@ q AND tenant_id = $3\n  ORDER BY ts_rank_cd(tsv, q) DESC LIMIT 50\n)\nSELECT COALESCE(s.id, k.id) AS id,\n       COALESCE(1.0 / (60 + s.rank), 0) + COALESCE(1.0 / (60 + k.rank), 0) AS rrf_score\nFROM semantic s FULL OUTER JOIN keyword k ON s.id = k.id\nORDER BY rrf_score DESC LIMIT 10;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>"}]);
